  "result": "Fixed. The command now prints `27.06`.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded the decimal part on the wrong side. It used `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`, which turns `\"12.5\"` into `\"05\"`, so the price became 1205 cents instead of 1250. That gave (1205 \u00d7 2) plus 8.25% tax = 26.09. With the right price it's (1250 \u00d7 2) plus 8.25% tax = 27.06.\n\n**Fix:** I changed it to `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")` so that `.5` becomes `50`. I also dropped the `or 0` fallback, because it never did anything: the padded string is never empty. A quick check: `to_cents` now gives `'12'`\u21921200, `'12.5'`\u21921250, `'12.05'`\u21921205 and `'0.5'`\u219250.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`. All 6 pass. None of them test `to_cents` directly, which is how this bug got past them. A test for `to_cents` with one decimal place would be worth adding.\n\nThere's also no `shop/__main__.py`, so `python3 -m shop` doesn't work; I used the `python3 -c` form you gave.",
